{
  "0": "Across all four datasets, reduce the pointwise logistic coefficient from 0.2 to 0.05: softplus(negative-positive)+0.05*[softplus(-positive)+softplus(negative)], retaining the existing nonpadding reduction. Weaker absolute-score supervision may improve ranking with untied normalized embeddings. Preserve sampling, GRU topology, scoring, optimizer and training settings.",
  "1": "Across all four datasets, jointly set maxlen=48, learning rate=0.0004 and dropout=0.10. Most histories fit within 48 positions; shorter inputs reduce padding computation, while smaller updates and less dropout may stabilize learning of the untied normalized embeddings. Preserve hidden width, depth, embedding design, loss, readout, batch size and training caps; save maxlen in predictor metadata.",
  "2": "Across all four datasets, replace each GRU state's readout with 0.75 times that state plus 0.25 times a causal, exponentially weighted mean of nonpadding GRU states through that position, using half-life 3 interactions. Apply this identically at training positions and the final real inference position. Recent-state pooling may retain useful short-term interests without learned pooling parameters. Preserve history length, GRU backbone, embeddings, scoring, loss and optimizer; update the predictor writer consistently.",
  "3": "Across all four datasets, test mild positive frequency scaling of history embeddings: multiply by clamp((max(training_item_count,1)/median_positive_count)^0.10,0.9,1.1), keeping padding zero. Previous inverse-frequency scaling regressed; modestly emphasizing frequent observed items may provide more reliable recurrent inputs. Compute counts solely from training interactions and checkpoint the scales for identical inference. Preserve learned embeddings, GRU topology, readout, candidate scoring, loss and optimizer."
}
